### My answer to the prompt

```html
<x-details>
  <span slot="header">Header here</span>
  <div slot="intro">Intro part always visible</div>
  <div slot="details">Details part — visible when expanded</div>
</x-details>

<script>
  class XDetails extends HTMLElement {
    constructor() {
      super();
      this.attachShadow({ mode: "open" });

      this.shadowRoot.innerHTML = `
        <style>
          :host { display: block; }

          .header {
            display: flex;
            align-items: center;
            gap: 0.5rem;
            padding-bottom: 0.4rem;
            margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
            border-bottom: 1px solid #ccc;
            font-size: 1.2rem;
          }

          .toggle {
            cursor: pointer;
          }

          .indicator {
            display: inline-grid;
            place-items: center;
            width: 1.2rem;
            height: 1.2rem;
            border: 1px solid currentColor;
            box-sizing: border-box;
          }

          .intro { margin-bottom: 0.6rem; }
          button { cursor: pointer; }
        </style>

        <div class="header">
          <button class="toggle" type="button" aria-expanded="false">
            <span class="indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
            <slot name="header"></slot>
          </button>
        </div>
        <div class="intro"><slot name="intro"></slot></div>
        <div class="details" hidden><slot name="details"></slot></div>
        <button class="more" type="button">expand more</button>
      `;
    }

    connectedCallback() {
      this.toggleButton = this.shadowRoot.querySelector(".toggle");
      this.indicator = this.shadowRoot.querySelector(".indicator");
      this.details = this.shadowRoot.querySelector(".details");
      this.moreButton = this.shadowRoot.querySelector(".more");

      this.toggleButton.addEventListener("click", () => this.toggle());
      this.moreButton.addEventListener("click", () => this.toggle());
    }

    toggle() {
      const expanded = this.details.hidden;
      this.details.hidden = !expanded;
      this.toggleButton.setAttribute("aria-expanded", String(expanded));
      this.indicator.textContent = expanded ? "−" : "+";
      this.moreButton.textContent = expanded ? "collapse" : "expand more";
    }
  }

  customElements.define("x-details", XDetails);
</script>
```

### Evaluation

Assistant A is closer to a working answer. It provides the requested slots, Shadow DOM, styling, collapsed intro, and an “expand more” button. However, it attaches click handlers to both the indicator and its containing header. Clicking the indicator triggers both handlers as the click bubbles, toggling twice and leaving the component in its original state. The header and “expand more” button still work.

Assistant B has more substantial problems. The indicator never displays `+` or `−`, as requested. The “expand more” button is hidden initially, even though it should be visible while collapsed. Also, `setAttribute('open', false)` leaves an `open` attribute present (with the string value `"false"`), so the CSS attribute selectors continue treating the elements as open after toggling back.

Both answers offer a relevant implementation, but A satisfies more of the requested behavior and is easier to correct. Neither includes a functional `+`/`−` indicator throughout the interaction, and A’s indicator click bug is important, but B misses multiple core requirements.

My final verdict is Assistant A is slightly better: [[A>B]]

